# hello_exec manifest — Uros #235 pilot.
#
# hello_exec is the canonical exec_server smoke binary: bare _start
# that walks the AUXV and loops on mach_null.  It never calls
# cap_request, so what it asks for here it never uses.

name hello_exec

# ── And yet it asks (#743) ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Block devices READ|WRITE (4, any, 0x3) and DMA buffers (10, any, 0x3),
# which nothing that execs it may hand on in full.
#
# 🔴 UNTIL #743 THIS FILE WAS THE PROGRAM'S POLICY.  exec_server
# provisioned whatever manifest the binary carried, so these two lines
# would have been granted as written: whoever writes a binary that
# something execs wrote what it may do.
#
# 🔑 Now the program gets what it asks for cut to what its caller's
# manifest may hand on.  cap_test [42] execs it with a manifest that hands
# on block devices READ alone, and its port must grant that and refuse
# WRITE and DMA buffers.  A caller with no per-task port gives it nothing.
required 4 any 0x3
required 10 any 0x3

# No `delegatable` lines.
